WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:02.367
-Wow, in person!
-In person.

00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:04.133
Thank you for being here.
I missed seeing you.

00:00:04.133 --> 00:00:05.667
We were Zooming.
-Yeah, well, I miss --

00:00:05.667 --> 00:00:08.200
I miss you not being able
to say "unmute" to me.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.300
I mean, you know.
-[ Laughs ] Exactly.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.701
Do you always get a long
standing ovation like that?

00:00:12.701 --> 00:00:14.400
What's the longest --
-At home. At home.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.000
-Wow.
-No, no. That's lovely.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.567
I more than appreciate it.

00:00:17.567 --> 00:00:18.834
I'm so glad to be here.

00:00:18.834 --> 00:00:20.634
-I was cheering you on.

00:00:20.634 --> 00:00:22.767
I was watching the Critics'
Choice Awards the other night,

00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:25.167
and you received
a Lifetime Achievement Award.

00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:27.701
-It was very nice. It was great.
-That was -- that was amazing.

00:00:27.701 --> 00:00:29.434
I mean, for all the things
you've done,

00:00:29.434 --> 00:00:30.834
that's got to feel great.

00:00:30.834 --> 00:00:32.801
-It was a little scary,
because when they call and say,

00:00:32.801 --> 00:00:35.634
"It's a lifetime achievement,"
do they know something I don't?

00:00:35.634 --> 00:00:37.367
-Yeah, exactly. Yeah.
-What's happening?

00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:39.133
Yeah, it was --
it was really nice to be

00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:41.501
in front of people again,

00:00:41.501 --> 00:00:43.834
you know,
in a beautiful setting,

00:00:43.834 --> 00:00:45.834
and people were
getting back to normal.

00:00:45.834 --> 00:00:47.033
-Yeah.
-And -- And that was --

00:00:47.033 --> 00:00:48.467
That was really nice.
-That was nice.

00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:52.100
I saw Jimmy Kimmel gave you --
gave you the award.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.767
-Yes, he made the speech.
-Yeah.

00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:56.467
-And it was -- he was great
because Jimmy's also terrific.

00:00:56.467 --> 00:00:57.801
-Yeah, he's fantastic.

00:00:57.801 --> 00:00:59.667
-And when I came out, I said,
"This is awkward. I --

00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:01.434
I had asked for Jimmy Fallon."

00:01:01.434 --> 00:01:03.767
[ Laughter ]

00:01:03.767 --> 00:01:05.501
Now it was, "What do I do?"
-It was really funny.

00:01:05.501 --> 00:01:06.767
-He's right over here.
What do I do?

00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:08.234
-"What do I do?"
Yeah, yeah. It was great.

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:10.434
It was perfect. He was good.
-But I remember -- you know,

00:01:10.434 --> 00:01:13.200
I heard you telling the story
about when we first met.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.767
And then you came over to --
We were shooting "Analyze This."

00:01:15.767 --> 00:01:17.701
And you came over to the hotel.

00:01:17.701 --> 00:01:19.334
-Yeah.
-And we talked for, like,

00:01:19.334 --> 00:01:22.901
two hours about characters
and what to do and what to --

00:01:22.901 --> 00:01:24.701
-You were so nice to me.
-And then here you are.

00:01:24.701 --> 00:01:26.634
-I will never forget
as long as I live,

00:01:26.634 --> 00:01:28.667
that you took the time
to even talk to me.

00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:30.634
You were like, "Jimmy, write.
Just keep writing."

00:01:30.634 --> 00:01:32.300
-Yeah.
-You gave me great advice.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.067
-And that's really --
You know what?

00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:37.200
Eventually, how this musical
ends up being,

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.000
because Buddy Young
was a character that I first did

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.801
on an HBO special in 1984 called
"A Comic's Line" where I --

00:01:44.801 --> 00:01:46.567
It was a parody
of "A Chorus Line,"

00:01:46.567 --> 00:01:48.167
so I played all these
different people.

00:01:48.167 --> 00:01:50.300
-Yeah.
-And he was one of them.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.567
And he was, like,
a hack comedian, you know?

00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:54.934
With a bad prom tuxedo.
-Yes.

00:01:54.934 --> 00:01:57.400
-"Hi. I'm Buddy Young Jr.
from Las Vegas, Nevada.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.934
And, boy, is my son stupid."

00:01:59.934 --> 00:02:01.868
Comes in with a lump of dog crap
in his hand.

00:02:01.868 --> 00:02:03.534
He said, "Pop, I almost
stepped in this."

00:02:03.534 --> 00:02:05.667
You know?
He's unbelievably dumb.

00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:09.334
But then when I brought him
here to "SNL"

00:02:09.334 --> 00:02:11.534
when I came here in 1984 --

00:02:11.534 --> 00:02:12.634
-Yeah. In this building.

00:02:12.634 --> 00:02:13.901
-Yeah, and then
we brought him here.

00:02:13.901 --> 00:02:15.434
And then I had him
at the news desk.

00:02:15.434 --> 00:02:18.033
He was a restaurant critic,
right?

00:02:18.033 --> 00:02:20.534
And then I did a special called
"Don't Get Me Started,"

00:02:20.534 --> 00:02:22.300
which I know you --
you happen to really love.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:23.634
-I think it's one
of the best --

00:02:23.634 --> 00:02:25.200
I think it's the best special
that's ever --

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.701
ever aired on HBO.
-Thanks. It was a lot of fun.

00:02:26.701 --> 00:02:28.501
-It was groundbreaking.
-And then we did the film,

00:02:28.501 --> 00:02:30.334
"Mr. Saturday Night," in 1991.

00:02:30.334 --> 00:02:32.234
So 30 years later,
you know, when I --

00:02:32.234 --> 00:02:34.868
When we did the movie,
I was -- I was 40.

00:02:34.868 --> 00:02:36.033
-Yeah.
-Right?

00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:37.767
And the character is 73
in the movie.

00:02:37.767 --> 00:02:40.701
So it was like five hours
of makeup every day and so on.

00:02:40.701 --> 00:02:43.334
So, now I turned 74 on Monday,

00:02:43.334 --> 00:02:45.033
so they have to make me
look younger.

00:02:45.033 --> 00:02:47.200
-Wow. See how that happens?
Isn't that amazing?

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.033
-This is a thrill.
I just show up now. Yeah.

00:02:49.033 --> 00:02:50.734
-By the way,
happy belated birthday.

00:02:50.734 --> 00:02:52.501
-Thank you.
-I -- Really, I --

00:02:52.501 --> 00:02:53.834
-Thank you.
-We love you.

00:02:53.834 --> 00:02:55.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:55.601 --> 00:02:57.667
-Believe me, it's my pleasure.

00:02:57.667 --> 00:02:59.667
-You share --
You share the birthday with --

00:02:59.667 --> 00:03:01.567
-My grandson Griffin.
-Yeah.

00:03:01.567 --> 00:03:04.167
-Yeah, he turned 9. I didn't.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:04.167 --> 00:03:07.934
And -- And, so, I had to fly
to Los Angeles for this award,

00:03:07.934 --> 00:03:10.734
and I didn't tell them
that I was coming.

00:03:10.734 --> 00:03:12.567
I wanted to surprise him.

00:03:12.567 --> 00:03:13.968
So, we're birthday buddies,

00:03:13.968 --> 00:03:15.667
and it means a lot
to both of us.

00:03:15.667 --> 00:03:18.667
So my -- my amazing wife,
Janice,

00:03:18.667 --> 00:03:22.901
gets this huge carton,
and we called him up,

00:03:22.901 --> 00:03:24.934
and I made-believe
I was at rehearsal in New York.

00:03:24.934 --> 00:03:26.734
And I said, "I've got to go,
but, you know,

00:03:26.734 --> 00:03:28.033
there's a big present for you.

00:03:28.033 --> 00:03:30.801
So it's on his front --
you know, so maybe --

00:03:30.801 --> 00:03:33.267
Go pick it up, and I love you
and I hope to see you.

00:03:33.267 --> 00:03:34.634
But I've got to go.
I'm in rehearsal!"

00:03:34.634 --> 00:03:36.534
I was, you know,
two minutes away.

00:03:36.534 --> 00:03:38.234
And, so, I got into the box.

00:03:38.234 --> 00:03:40.000
-This is insane.
-Yeah.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.868
And I heard him
coming up the steps.

00:03:41.868 --> 00:03:45.200
So here's the last moments of me
opening up the box

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:46.701
and I'm in it.

00:03:46.701 --> 00:03:48.267
-You videotaped his reaction?
-Yeah.

00:03:48.267 --> 00:03:49.968
Well, I had my phone
inside in the dark.

00:03:49.968 --> 00:03:51.767
-Here we go. Check this out.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:58.534
-[ Laughter ]
-Oh, my God.

00:03:58.534 --> 00:04:00.400
[ Woman screams ]

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:01.934
-Happy birthday!

00:04:01.934 --> 00:04:03.634
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:03.634 --> 00:04:05.801
[ Audience "Awws" ]

00:04:05.801 --> 00:04:08.334
-I mean, come on. Is that --
You can't pay for that.

00:04:08.334 --> 00:04:10.234
-No, no. It's cool.
-Is that the greatest --

00:04:10.234 --> 00:04:11.400
-Yeah.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:12.834
-That's a genuine laugh
right there.

00:04:12.834 --> 00:04:14.667
-Yeah. Yeah.
-It's so cool.

00:04:14.667 --> 00:04:17.634
And they're gonna --
Do they know your work?

00:04:17.634 --> 00:04:19.367
Do they -- Have they seen --
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:19.367 --> 00:04:21.133
They -- You know, they catch up

00:04:21.133 --> 00:04:23.267
because you can find anything,
you know?

00:04:23.267 --> 00:04:24.901
-Yeah, I guess.
Yeah, you're right.

00:04:24.901 --> 00:04:28.868
-And so he's a little -- he's
a little comedy nerd, Griffin.

00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:30.334
-Yeah. Good.
-He'll watch something,

00:04:30.334 --> 00:04:32.234
and he'll be like --
He'll be like me.

00:04:32.234 --> 00:04:35.667
And he -- he won't laugh much.
He'll just go, "Funny."

00:04:35.667 --> 00:04:37.567
[ Laughter ]
-He analyzed it.

00:04:37.567 --> 00:04:38.767
-"Funny."
-Yeah.

00:04:38.767 --> 00:04:41.167
-I thought I knew
everything you did.

00:04:41.167 --> 00:04:44.667
And, you know, I'm a student
of yours, and I love your stuff.

00:04:44.667 --> 00:04:46.133
But I -- You were telling me

00:04:46.133 --> 00:04:48.133
that Buddy Young Jr.
was on "Update."

00:04:48.133 --> 00:04:50.200
I'm like, "I don't remember..."
Because I remember him

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:51.801
from "Don't Get Me Started."

00:04:51.801 --> 00:04:53.734
But he was a restaurant critic?
-A restaurant critic.

00:04:53.734 --> 00:04:56.200
Yeah, Christopher Guest
was the host of "Update."

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:57.501
-That's right.

00:04:57.501 --> 00:04:59.434
-And so Buddy now
was an insult comic.

00:04:59.434 --> 00:05:01.434
But I would do
these restaurant jokes.

00:05:01.434 --> 00:05:02.868
-So he would roast people.
-Roast people.

00:05:02.868 --> 00:05:05.234
So then Dick Ebersol
produced it that year for us.

00:05:05.234 --> 00:05:07.467
Said, "We have some famous
people in the audience.

00:05:07.467 --> 00:05:09.133
What do you want to do?
Do you want to...?"

00:05:09.133 --> 00:05:11.834
I said, "I'll just go
after them. Who is it?"

00:05:11.834 --> 00:05:14.367
So we're just live,
and it happened to be the one

00:05:14.367 --> 00:05:16.501
and only Johnny Cash
was in the audience.

00:05:16.501 --> 00:05:18.901
So here's a little clip
from 37 years ago from two --

00:05:18.901 --> 00:05:20.434
two stories up.
-And this is live.

00:05:20.434 --> 00:05:22.234
No script, no new --
-No, no, no, I just go.

00:05:22.234 --> 00:05:23.734
-Billy Crystal, check this out.

00:05:23.734 --> 00:05:26.501
-And you must be, uh...
-Johnny Cash.

00:05:26.501 --> 00:05:36.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:36.501 --> 00:05:37.834
-Who cares?
You know what I'm saying?

00:05:37.834 --> 00:05:39.267
[ Laughter ]

00:05:39.267 --> 00:05:41.834
Good to see you working with
new colors these days, Johnny.

00:05:41.834 --> 00:05:45.367
Well, you believe, he's got
the Portuguese flag

00:05:45.367 --> 00:05:46.701
around his throat.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:46.701 --> 00:05:48.267
Johnny,
from the bottom of my heart,

00:05:48.267 --> 00:05:49.434
it's over.
You know what I'm saying?

00:05:49.434 --> 00:05:50.734
[ Laughter ]

00:05:50.734 --> 00:05:52.133
No, seriously.
I kid, Johnny.

00:05:52.133 --> 00:05:53.234
He's a big star.

00:05:53.234 --> 00:05:55.033
Does Jack Lord know
you have his hair?

00:05:55.033 --> 00:05:56.334
[ Laughter ]

00:05:56.334 --> 00:05:58.200
We need love today.
Oh, excuse me.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.400
Oh, I was almost Jewish again.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:04.234
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:06:04.234 --> 00:06:07.133
-Oh, my God.
You --

00:06:07.133 --> 00:06:09.567
-Yeah.
-You don't get intimidated

00:06:09.567 --> 00:06:11.334
when you see Johnny Cash --
and Waylon Jennings

00:06:11.334 --> 00:06:12.801
sitting right next to him?
-Yeah, there's Waylon Jennings,

00:06:12.801 --> 00:06:14.300
so I said,
"When did you go Hasid?

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:15.767
I didn't know
that you went Hasid."

00:06:15.767 --> 00:06:16.868
Because he had this big hat on,

00:06:16.868 --> 00:06:18.601
and Christopher Reeve
was there, too.

00:06:18.601 --> 00:06:20.400
And, you know,
just loose and just fun.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:23.534
And so, then, you know, this
character just stays with me.

00:06:23.534 --> 00:06:25.033
And so, we did the film.

00:06:25.033 --> 00:06:28.934
But then Mel Brooks
called me in 2004

00:06:28.934 --> 00:06:30.968
when I was finishing up
my run of "700 Sundays."

00:06:30.968 --> 00:06:32.467
-God, you were great.
But, like -- But I know,

00:06:32.467 --> 00:06:34.033
can we just say for a second
how great you were

00:06:34.033 --> 00:06:35.868
in "700 Sundays" on Broadway?

00:06:35.868 --> 00:06:37.400
One of my favorite shows
I ever saw.

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:38.701
-Oh, thank you.
Thanks.

00:06:38.701 --> 00:06:40.467
-I remember, I saw it twice,

00:06:40.467 --> 00:06:42.067
because I was like,
"I have to do this again.

00:06:42.067 --> 00:06:43.067
I have to experience it."

00:06:43.067 --> 00:06:45.167
It was so funny but emotional.

00:06:45.167 --> 00:06:47.200
And it was --
it was just a fun night,

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:48.400
because --
-Yeah.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:49.601
-Do you remember --
I was there that night.

00:06:49.601 --> 00:06:51.200
-And Sandy Koufax was there
with Joe Torre.

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:52.634
-Yeah, it was crazy.

00:06:52.634 --> 00:06:54.534
-Yeah, and we went out for,
like, till 3:00 in the morning

00:06:54.534 --> 00:06:55.968
just talking
about baseball and stuff.

00:06:55.968 --> 00:06:57.367
It was, like, the greatest.
Yeah.

00:06:57.367 --> 00:06:59.634
-Do you like having people
visit you backstage

00:06:59.634 --> 00:07:01.167
and say hi? Okay.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:01.167 --> 00:07:04.834
There's only one person who
ever disappointed me backstage.

00:07:04.834 --> 00:07:06.667
-Who?
-The Geico gecko.

00:07:06.667 --> 00:07:08.534
-Oh.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:08.534 --> 00:07:09.767
-But, you know,
it's interesting.

00:07:09.767 --> 00:07:12.300
Because first of all,
he's a little arrogant.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:13.567
[ Laughter ]
-Wow.

00:07:13.567 --> 00:07:14.901
-With the accent
and the whole thing.

00:07:14.901 --> 00:07:17.467
And it's like --
it's like meeting Tom Cruise.

00:07:17.467 --> 00:07:19.400
He's a little smaller in person.
-Oh!

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:20.701
[ Laughter ]
That's very interesting.

00:07:20.701 --> 00:07:22.200
-And -- yeah,
and he's like about this.

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:23.667
-Yeah.
-And he hands me a script.

00:07:23.667 --> 00:07:24.934
"Could you read it?"

00:07:24.934 --> 00:07:26.267
-No, you don't have time
for this stuff.

00:07:26.267 --> 00:07:28.100
I'm sorry to hear that.
-No, so Mel Brooks calls me

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:32.634
at the end of the run,
and he says, "Listen, Billy.

00:07:32.634 --> 00:07:35.767
I got two ideas.
Here's the first.

00:07:35.767 --> 00:07:37.701
I'd like you to be
Max Bialystock

00:07:37.701 --> 00:07:39.501
in 'The Producers.'
Come across the street.

00:07:39.501 --> 00:07:40.667
You'll do it for six months.

00:07:40.667 --> 00:07:43.067
Whatever you want to do,
it's yours."

00:07:43.067 --> 00:07:45.167
So I said, "Mel, I love you.

00:07:45.167 --> 00:07:46.801
I don't want to be
the fifth person

00:07:46.801 --> 00:07:48.567
to play Max Bialystock."
-Yeah.

00:07:48.567 --> 00:07:50.234
-"You won't,
you'll be the 12th."

00:07:50.234 --> 00:07:52.000
[ Laughter ]

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:53.701
And then this company --

00:07:53.701 --> 00:07:55.934
these people in London said,
"Would 'Mr. Saturday Night'

00:07:55.934 --> 00:07:57.300
be a musical, you think?"

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:00.567
So I started thinking
about it then, put it away.

00:08:00.567 --> 00:08:03.868
Then over time, I went back
to our original writers,

00:08:03.868 --> 00:08:05.667
who you know very well --
Lowell Ganz and Babaloo Mandel.

00:08:05.667 --> 00:08:07.200
-Babaloo Mandel.
-The best.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:09.601
And we wrote the movie together.

00:08:09.601 --> 00:08:11.801
And we started fashioning
the musical,

00:08:11.801 --> 00:08:14.234
and it got better and better
and better and better.

00:08:14.234 --> 00:08:17.200
And here we're going to
start previews March 29th,

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:18.968
and we open April 27th.

00:08:18.968 --> 00:08:20.300
I hope you can make that.
-Of course.

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:23.868
-The show, I have to say, is --
it's hilarious.

00:08:23.868 --> 00:08:24.934
It's really touching.

00:08:24.934 --> 00:08:26.367
The music is phenomenal.

00:08:26.367 --> 00:08:29.100
A great composer --
Jason Robert Brown --

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:31.334
and Amanda Green
wrote the lyrics.

00:08:31.334 --> 00:08:33.601
And it's beyond --
it's the best version

00:08:33.601 --> 00:08:34.968
of this character, because --

00:08:34.968 --> 00:08:37.033
-Have you ever sang on --
-Yeah, well -- you know,

00:08:37.033 --> 00:08:40.501
I sang the last time in college
in "Finian's Rainbow."

00:08:40.501 --> 00:08:41.734
I was the leprechaun.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:41.734 --> 00:08:43.701
-Wow.
-Perfect timing for today.

00:08:43.701 --> 00:08:45.300
A Jewish leprechaun.
-Happy St. Patrick's day, yeah.

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:47.734
-And --
And then on the Oscars.

00:08:47.734 --> 00:08:49.434
I would sing
when I was on the Oscars.

00:08:49.434 --> 00:08:51.634
But this is --
I've been training for a year.

00:08:51.634 --> 00:08:54.334
And I have like eight songs,
the music is spectacular.

00:08:54.334 --> 00:08:55.701
It's hilarious.

00:08:55.701 --> 00:08:57.601
And, boy, I couldn't be
more excited about --

00:08:57.601 --> 00:09:00.267
I'm going to be here open-ended.
So I'm -- I'm here.

00:09:00.267 --> 00:09:02.601
-Oh, but I'm -- but you're
gonna get tired of me.

00:09:02.601 --> 00:09:05.601
Buddy -- Buddy Young Jr.,
the story is basically --

00:09:05.601 --> 00:09:07.734
he's, like, a comic
who's kind of --

00:09:07.734 --> 00:09:10.334
-He's down and out, but he's --
he's not doing well.

00:09:10.334 --> 00:09:13.367
He had some problems
in the '50s,

00:09:13.367 --> 00:09:15.801
mentally, and his show
got cancelled.

00:09:15.801 --> 00:09:20.100
And now, present day, 1994,
in the show,

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:22.300
he's got a chance
to make a comeback.

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:25.634
And so now he gets his brother
back, who is his manager,

00:09:25.634 --> 00:09:27.701
played once again
by David Paymer.

00:09:27.701 --> 00:09:28.534
-That's --
-Who was nominated

00:09:28.534 --> 00:09:29.901
for an Oscar for the movie.

00:09:29.901 --> 00:09:31.934
-That's really good.
-For best supporting actor.

00:09:31.934 --> 00:09:33.200
We have an amazing cast.

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:36.467
Shoshana Bean, Randy Graff,
Chasten Harmon,

00:09:36.467 --> 00:09:38.167
and it's tremendous.

00:09:38.167 --> 00:09:42.701
And in getting his comeback
going, he realizes his family

00:09:42.701 --> 00:09:46.000
is suffering, and they don't --
they don't have enough of him.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:48.601
So, it really becomes
about pulling his --

00:09:48.601 --> 00:09:52.434
his estranged daughter back in
the fold and healing his family.

00:09:52.434 --> 00:09:55.033
So it's got a lot of stuff
in it that's beautiful.

00:09:55.033 --> 00:09:57.267
-I can't wait and --
-Me either.

00:09:57.267 --> 00:09:58.334
-And, really, I will be there.

00:09:58.334 --> 00:09:59.767
"Mr. Saturday Night" --
break a leg.

00:09:59.767 --> 00:10:01.167
Billy Crystal, everybody.

00:10:01.167 --> 00:10:02.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:02.334 --> 00:10:04.267
"Mr. Saturday Night"
begins previews

00:10:04.267 --> 00:10:07.067
March 29th on Broadway.

00:10:07.067 --> 00:10:08.801
Billy and I are doing something
fun after the break.

00:10:08.801 --> 00:10:10.801
Come on back, everybody.
It's good, it's good.

